1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:07,246
350 metres below the surface, a wellhead
is being bent back and forth.

2
00:00:07,266 --> 00:00:11,845
Once with every wave, for as long as the
drilling riser is connected.

3
00:00:12,665 --> 00:00:14,922
That's more than 10,000 load cycles a day.

4
00:00:14,942 --> 00:00:17,471
None of them comes close to
breaking the steel.

5
00:00:18,191 --> 00:00:20,708
But every cycle leaves a
little damage behind.

6
00:00:20,728 --> 00:00:24,458
Add up enough of them, and a crack grows
through the wall of a weld.

7
00:00:25,053 --> 00:00:28,258
Down here, nobody will ever
inspect those welds.

8
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:33,230
So we have to predict how long they will
last before the rig even arrives.

9
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:38,806
In this video, we'll do exactly that, step by
step, with the method in DNV-RP-C203.

10
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:44,975
First, a quick refresher.

11
00:00:44,995 --> 00:00:49,847
Fatigue is driven by stress cycles, and what
matters most is the stress range:

12
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:54,844
the difference between the highest and the
lowest stress in a cycle, written Δσ.

13
00:00:55,464 --> 00:00:58,679
In welded steel, cracks almost
always start at a weld.

14
00:00:58,699 --> 00:01:02,094
The weld toe is a sharp notch,
welding leaves tiny flaws,

15
00:01:02,114 --> 00:01:04,310
and residual stresses close to yield.

16
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:09,282
So a crack starts early, and most of the life is
spent growing it through the wall.

17
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:14,734
To measure fatigue strength, we test welded
specimens at a constant stress range

18
00:01:14,754 --> 00:01:16,675
and count the cycles to failure.

19
00:01:16,695 --> 00:01:21,223
Repeat that at several stress ranges, and
plot the results on log-log axes.

20
00:01:21,243 --> 00:01:24,618
They fall around a straight line,
with a lot of scatter.

21
00:01:25,237 --> 00:01:32,593
DNV-RP-C203 gives design S-N curves that sit two
standard deviations below the mean

22
00:01:32,613 --> 00:01:34,104
of the test data.

23
00:01:34,124 --> 00:01:38,102
That's a survival probability
of about 97.7%.

24
00:01:38,723 --> 00:01:40,353
The curve is a power law:

25
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:43,587
the number of cycles to failure
is a constant, ā,

26
00:01:43,607 --> 00:01:46,425
divided by the stress range
to the power m.

27
00:01:46,445 --> 00:01:47,943
For most welds, m is 3.

28
00:01:47,963 --> 00:01:52,365
So if you double the stress range, the
life drops by a factor of 8.

29
00:01:52,385 --> 00:01:55,599
Small errors in stress become
big errors in life.

30
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:00,773
Real loading is nothing like a
constant-amplitude test.

31
00:02:00,793 --> 00:02:04,053
So C203 turns the problem into
a chain of steps.

32
00:02:04,673 --> 00:02:07,303
Describe the sea states the well will see.

33
00:02:07,323 --> 00:02:10,709
Turn each one into a stress
history at every hot spot.

34
00:02:10,729 --> 00:02:12,223
Count the stress cycles.

35
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:13,799
Pick the right S-N curve.

36
00:02:13,819 --> 00:02:16,070
Add up the damage with Miner's rule.

37
00:02:16,090 --> 00:02:19,476
And finally, check it against a
design fatigue factor.

38
00:02:20,096 --> 00:02:24,457
In the standard itself, the method is
spread over a few chapters.

39
00:02:24,477 --> 00:02:27,827
Section two holds Miner's rule
and the S-N curves,

40
00:02:27,847 --> 00:02:30,859
with the rules for thickness
and mean stress.

41
00:02:30,879 --> 00:02:34,162
Section three gives stress
concentration factors,

42
00:02:34,182 --> 00:02:38,475
and section four the hot spot stress
from finite element models.

43
00:02:38,495 --> 00:02:43,395
Section five covers the fatigue analysis,
including the Weibull shortcut,

44
00:02:43,415 --> 00:02:45,956
and Appendix A classifies the details.

45
00:02:45,976 --> 00:02:48,517
The loads come from a global analysis,

46
00:02:48,537 --> 00:02:51,819
which for a wellhead is
described in DNV-RP-E104.

47
00:02:51,839 --> 00:02:55,930
And the design fatigue factor comes
from the design standard,

48
00:02:55,950 --> 00:02:57,547
for example DNV-OS-C101.

49
00:02:58,266 --> 00:03:00,800
Every link in that chain can
change the answer,

50
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:04,006
so let's go through them one at a
time, on a real wellhead.

51
00:03:05,833 --> 00:03:06,742
Here's our well:

52
00:03:06,762 --> 00:03:11,442
the same template, conductor and wellhead
housings we modelled in our last video.

53
00:03:11,462 --> 00:03:14,459
The riser pulls on the top of the
blowout preventer,

54
00:03:14,479 --> 00:03:16,606
and that force bends the whole stack.

55
00:03:17,226 --> 00:03:20,138
The global beam model shows
where the bending goes.

56
00:03:20,158 --> 00:03:22,725
Most of the moment passes
into the conductor,

57
00:03:22,745 --> 00:03:27,438
and it keeps growing below the seabed, to a peak
about 3 metres below the mudline.

58
00:03:28,059 --> 00:03:31,825
Fatigue cracks start where a high stress
range meets a weak detail.

59
00:03:31,845 --> 00:03:34,029
We'll follow three candidate hot spots:

60
00:03:34,049 --> 00:03:37,758
the weld between the high-pressure
housing and the surface casing,

61
00:03:37,778 --> 00:03:40,979
the weld between the conductor
housing and the conductor,

62
00:03:40,999 --> 00:03:44,483
and a girth weld in the conductor, 3
metres below the mudline,

63
00:03:44,503 --> 00:03:46,121
right where the moment peaks.

64
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:48,573
Step 1:

65
00:03:48,593 --> 00:03:49,099
the sea.

66
00:03:49,119 --> 00:03:54,355
A scatter diagram tells us how often each
combination of significant wave height

67
00:03:54,375 --> 00:03:55,866
and peak period occurs.

68
00:03:55,886 --> 00:03:57,509
Each cell is a sea state:

69
00:03:57,529 --> 00:04:01,056
a few hours of irregular waves
with steady statistics.

70
00:04:01,676 --> 00:04:05,302
At this site, half the time the
waves are below 2 metres,

71
00:04:05,322 --> 00:04:08,820
and seas above 6 metres occur less
than 2% of the time.

72
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:12,956
Within a sea state, the surface is a
sum of many sine waves,

73
00:04:12,976 --> 00:04:16,315
with the energy spread over
frequency by a wave spectrum.

74
00:04:16,935 --> 00:04:21,533
A global riser analysis turns those waves
into the force at the flex joint,

75
00:04:21,553 --> 00:04:23,503
on top of the blowout preventer.

76
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:26,951
Here we stand in for it with a
simple transfer function,

77
00:04:26,971 --> 00:04:29,167
so the example stays self-contained.

78
00:04:29,187 --> 00:04:32,245
The result is a force history
for every sea state.

79
00:04:34,267 --> 00:04:34,672
Step 2:

80
00:04:34,692 --> 00:04:35,946
from force to stress.

81
00:04:35,966 --> 00:04:39,648
We push the global model with an
increasing flex-joint force,

82
00:04:39,668 --> 00:04:42,015
and record the moment at each hot spot.

83
00:04:42,035 --> 00:04:45,354
These load-to-moment curves are
our transfer functions.

84
00:04:45,974 --> 00:04:47,557
They aren't straight lines.

85
00:04:47,577 --> 00:04:52,188
The soil springs soften, the radial clearance
between the two housings closes,

86
00:04:52,208 --> 00:04:55,572
and above about 50 kN the gap to
the template closes too,

87
00:04:55,592 --> 00:04:58,541
and the template starts to carry
part of the load.

88
00:04:59,161 --> 00:05:02,069
So the stress isn't simply
proportional to the force.

89
00:05:02,089 --> 00:05:06,103
We keep the full nonlinear curve, and run
every force history through it.

90
00:05:07,933 --> 00:05:09,275
From moment to stress.

91
00:05:09,295 --> 00:05:14,039
In a pipe under bending, the axial stress
varies linearly across the section,

92
00:05:14,059 --> 00:05:17,319
from tension on one side to
compression on the other.

93
00:05:17,339 --> 00:05:21,093
Its largest value, at the outer
fibre, is the nominal stress.

94
00:05:21,713 --> 00:05:26,049
It is the bending moment, M, divided
by the section modulus, W.

95
00:05:26,069 --> 00:05:29,644
The section modulus is the second
moment of area, I,

96
00:05:29,664 --> 00:05:32,617
divided by the distance to
the outer fibre:

97
00:05:32,637 --> 00:05:34,277
half the outer diameter.

98
00:05:34,297 --> 00:05:38,978
For a tube, I is π/64, times the outer
diameter to the fourth power,

99
00:05:38,998 --> 00:05:41,674
minus the inner diameter to the fourth.

100
00:05:42,293 --> 00:05:50,600
For the 36-inch conductor, with an outer
diameter of 914 mm and a 38.1 mm wall,

101
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:52,076
W is 0.022 m³.

102
00:05:52,096 --> 00:06:00,086
At 25 kN of flex-joint force, the moment at
the girth weld is about 430 kNm.

103
00:06:00,106 --> 00:06:05,356
Divided by W, that's a nominal
stress of 19.4 MPa.

104
00:06:05,976 --> 00:06:11,114
Because the relation is linear, a range of
moment gives a range of stress:

105
00:06:11,134 --> 00:06:11,811
Δσ = ΔM/W.

106
00:06:11,831 --> 00:06:17,248
Loads that stay constant, like the weight of
the stack, shift the mean stress,

107
00:06:17,268 --> 00:06:19,478
but they don't change the range.

108
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:24,031
The nominal stress assumes a smooth pipe.

109
00:06:24,051 --> 00:06:27,252
At a weld, the geometry
concentrates the stress.

110
00:06:27,272 --> 00:06:30,070
C203 calls the result the hot spot stress:

111
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:33,827
the nominal stress, times a stress
concentration factor.

112
00:06:34,447 --> 00:06:37,976
Through the wall at the weld toe, the
stress has three parts.

113
00:06:37,996 --> 00:06:40,768
A membrane part, constant
through the thickness.

114
00:06:40,788 --> 00:06:42,688
A bending part, varying linearly.

115
00:06:42,708 --> 00:06:46,295
And a sharp, nonlinear peak from the
notch of the weld itself.

116
00:06:46,315 --> 00:06:48,913
The hot spot stress is the
structural stress:

117
00:06:48,933 --> 00:06:51,123
membrane plus bending, at the surface.

118
00:06:51,143 --> 00:06:53,334
The notch peak is left out on purpose,

119
00:06:53,354 --> 00:06:56,592
because its effect is already in
the hot spot S-N curve.

120
00:06:57,212 --> 00:07:01,067
For the two housing welds, we compute it
with the local solid model.

121
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:05,340
Read the surface stress at half a wall
thickness and at one and a half wall

122
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:07,050
thicknesses from the weld toe.

123
00:07:07,070 --> 00:07:11,608
These points lie outside the notch peak, where
the stress still varies smoothly.

124
00:07:12,228 --> 00:07:14,734
Then extrapolate linearly to the toe.

125
00:07:14,754 --> 00:07:20,197
The straight line through the two points
reaches the toe at one and a half times

126
00:07:20,217 --> 00:07:22,928
the first stress, minus half the second.

127
00:07:22,948 --> 00:07:26,546
At the high-pressure housing
weld, that gives 35 MPa.

128
00:07:26,566 --> 00:07:31,872
Divided by the nominal stress of 21.9, the
stress concentration factor is 1.6.

129
00:07:32,592 --> 00:07:37,072
At the conductor housing weld, the outside is
actually less stressed than the

130
00:07:37,092 --> 00:07:37,890
nominal value.

131
00:07:37,910 --> 00:07:41,981
The housing thickens inwards, so the
wall's mid-surface steps inwards,

132
00:07:42,001 --> 00:07:44,319
and the axial stress adds local bending.

133
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:46,598
The inside of the weld is the hot spot.

134
00:07:46,618 --> 00:07:50,981
That assumes the weld is made from both
sides, so the inside is a weld toe.

135
00:07:51,001 --> 00:07:55,422
Welded from the outside only, the inside
would be a weld root, an F3 detail,

136
00:07:55,442 --> 00:07:57,234
with less than 50 days allowed.

137
00:07:57,254 --> 00:07:58,812
Always check both surfaces.

138
00:07:59,432 --> 00:08:01,954
And the conductor wall is thick.

139
00:08:01,974 --> 00:08:07,276
For thick walls, C203 recommends a
quadratic extrapolation instead,

140
00:08:07,296 --> 00:08:11,406
through points at 0.4, 0.9 and
1.4 wall thicknesses:

141
00:08:11,426 --> 00:08:12,439
equation 4.9.

142
00:08:12,459 --> 00:08:17,126
Here it gives 8% more than the
straight line, so we use it:

143
00:08:17,146 --> 00:08:20,144
a stress concentration factor of 1.24.

144
00:08:20,764 --> 00:08:21,510
One caveat.

145
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:24,155
Our model has perfectly aligned walls.

146
00:08:24,175 --> 00:08:29,167
At butt welds, C203 requires fabrication
tolerances to be accounted for.

147
00:08:29,187 --> 00:08:34,109
We assume the housing welds stay within
the 5% already in the S-N data:

148
00:08:34,129 --> 00:08:37,520
under 1 mm on the casing, and 2
on the conductor.

149
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:40,861
Anything beyond that would need
an extra factor.

150
00:08:42,667 --> 00:08:46,353
The conductor girth weld is a
standard butt weld in a tube.

151
00:08:46,373 --> 00:08:48,678
So instead of a finite element model,

152
00:08:48,698 --> 00:08:53,264
C203 gives a formula for the stress
concentration that matters most here:

153
00:08:53,284 --> 00:08:54,081
misalignment.

154
00:08:54,701 --> 00:08:57,062
When the two pipe ends don't line up,

155
00:08:57,082 --> 00:09:00,989
the axial stress has to jog sideways
by the misalignment, δm.

156
00:09:01,009 --> 00:09:03,629
That eccentricity bends the wall locally,

157
00:09:03,649 --> 00:09:06,590
and adds bending stress right
at the weld toe.

158
00:09:07,211 --> 00:09:09,251
This is equation 3.9.

159
00:09:09,271 --> 00:09:17,001
The SCF is one, plus three times δm minus δ0,
divided by the wall thickness, t,

160
00:09:17,021 --> 00:09:18,080
times e^−α.

161
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:21,422
The one is the stress
without misalignment.

162
00:09:21,442 --> 00:09:23,781
The second term is the extra bending.

163
00:09:23,801 --> 00:09:28,117
An eccentricity e in a wall of thickness
t creates a bending moment.

164
00:09:28,137 --> 00:09:30,986
The pipe on both sides of the
weld shares it,

165
00:09:31,006 --> 00:09:35,960
and the bending stress at the surface becomes
3e/t, times the membrane stress.

166
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:42,410
δ0 is the misalignment already present in the
test specimens behind the S-N curve,

167
00:09:42,430 --> 00:09:43,837
so it is subtracted.

168
00:09:43,857 --> 00:09:49,545
For girth welds in tubular structures, C203
sets it to 5% of the wall thickness,

169
00:09:49,565 --> 00:09:52,043
and to zero for welds ground flush.

170
00:09:52,663 --> 00:09:54,484
Alpha brings in the pipe.

171
00:09:54,504 --> 00:09:58,387
It is 0.91 × L, the width of the
weld at the surface,

172
00:09:58,407 --> 00:10:01,038
divided by the square root of D × t.

173
00:10:01,058 --> 00:10:06,488
A wider weld takes out the eccentricity more
gradually, and the SCF drops.

174
00:10:07,109 --> 00:10:08,757
For our weld:

175
00:10:08,777 --> 00:10:17,995
6 mm of hi-lo, a 38.1 mm wall, a 40 mm wide
weld cap, and a 914 mm pipe.

176
00:10:18,015 --> 00:10:24,795
Alpha is 0.195, so e^−α is 0.82,
and the SCF is 1.27.

177
00:10:25,415 --> 00:10:27,608
One more detail, from Appendix B.

178
00:10:27,628 --> 00:10:32,706
Under global bending, the local bending is
driven by the stress at mid-wall,

179
00:10:32,726 --> 00:10:35,859
which here is 4% lower than at
the outer fibre.

180
00:10:35,879 --> 00:10:41,158
So the stress at the outside toe is the
outer-fibre stress, plus SCF minus one,

181
00:10:41,178 --> 00:10:42,902
times the mid-wall stress.

182
00:10:42,922 --> 00:10:45,384
That's 1.25 times the nominal stress.

183
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:49,451
Step 3:

184
00:10:49,471 --> 00:10:50,529
counting cycles.

185
00:10:50,549 --> 00:10:52,885
A real stress history is irregular.

186
00:10:52,905 --> 00:10:58,473
Small cycles ride on top of bigger ones, and
it's not obvious what a cycle even is.

187
00:10:59,093 --> 00:11:01,538
The standard answer is rainflow counting.

188
00:11:01,558 --> 00:11:05,987
Reduce the history to its peaks and valleys,
then read them one at a time,

189
00:11:06,007 --> 00:11:08,332
and keep comparing the last two ranges.

190
00:11:08,852 --> 00:11:12,662
Whenever the newest range is at least as
large as the one before it,

191
00:11:12,682 --> 00:11:14,296
the one before it is counted.

192
00:11:14,316 --> 00:11:18,689
If it contains the very first point of the
history, it counts as half a cycle,

193
00:11:18,709 --> 00:11:20,436
and only that point is dropped.

194
00:11:20,456 --> 00:11:22,182
Otherwise it is a closed cycle:

195
00:11:22,202 --> 00:11:24,773
count it, remove its two
points, and carry on.

196
00:11:24,793 --> 00:11:27,139
Each small cycle is paired off on its own,

197
00:11:27,159 --> 00:11:29,617
without breaking up the big
cycle around it.

198
00:11:30,237 --> 00:11:33,800
Whatever is left at the end is
counted as half cycles.

199
00:11:33,820 --> 00:11:37,117
For a 3-hour sea state, we get
about 1,400 cycles,

200
00:11:37,137 --> 00:11:39,837
sorted into a histogram of stress ranges.

201
00:11:40,457 --> 00:11:43,141
Notice that we count ranges,
not mean stress.

202
00:11:43,161 --> 00:11:45,846
Welds carry residual stresses
close to yield,

203
00:11:45,866 --> 00:11:50,353
so for as-welded joints C203 uses the full
stress range, whatever the mean.

204
00:11:50,373 --> 00:11:55,341
So the lock ring preload and the weight of the
stack don't enter the S-N check as a

205
00:11:55,361 --> 00:11:56,062
mean stress.

206
00:11:56,082 --> 00:11:57,624
They still matter, though:

207
00:11:57,644 --> 00:12:01,350
they set the contact and the load
sharing in the global model.

208
00:12:01,970 --> 00:12:03,323
There are exceptions.

209
00:12:03,343 --> 00:12:07,573
For base material, and for welds that
are post weld heat treated,

210
00:12:07,593 --> 00:12:12,738
C203 lets you reduce the stress range when
part of the cycle is in compression.

211
00:12:12,758 --> 00:12:17,707
For a stress-relieved weld at zero mean
stress, the reduction factor is 0.9.

212
00:12:17,727 --> 00:12:20,976
Our welds are as-welded, so the
full range counts.

213
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:23,266
Step 4:

214
00:12:23,286 --> 00:12:24,239
the S-N curve.

215
00:12:24,259 --> 00:12:29,033
C203 has a whole family of curves, from
B1 for plain, unwelded steel,

216
00:12:29,053 --> 00:12:32,646
down to W3 for cracks through the
weld metal itself.

217
00:12:32,666 --> 00:12:35,425
Which one applies depends on the detail:

218
00:12:35,445 --> 00:12:40,636
its geometry, how it's welded and inspected,
and which way the stress acts.

219
00:12:41,256 --> 00:12:43,941
Appendix A classifies a long
list of details.

220
00:12:43,961 --> 00:12:46,706
For a girth weld, it depends
on how it's made.

221
00:12:46,726 --> 00:12:50,253
Welded from both sides, in the shop,
with the pipe rotated,

222
00:12:50,273 --> 00:12:51,876
the weld toe is a D detail.

223
00:12:51,896 --> 00:12:53,680
Welded on site, it drops to E.

224
00:12:53,700 --> 00:12:56,625
And welded from one side only,
the root is an F3.

225
00:12:56,645 --> 00:12:59,451
We assume shop welds, so our
girth weld is a D.

226
00:12:59,471 --> 00:13:03,238
And when the hot spot stress comes
from a finite element model,

227
00:13:03,258 --> 00:13:05,462
C203 links it to the D curve as well.

228
00:13:06,082 --> 00:13:07,294
Then the environment.

229
00:13:07,314 --> 00:13:08,996
Seawater is harsher than air.

230
00:13:09,016 --> 00:13:13,866
With cathodic protection, the steep part of the
D curve drops by a factor of 2.5 in

231
00:13:13,886 --> 00:13:17,446
life, and the bend moves from 10
million cycles to 1 million.

232
00:13:17,466 --> 00:13:20,732
In free corrosion, the curve is a
factor of 3 below air,

233
00:13:20,752 --> 00:13:23,020
with a single slope and no bend at all.

234
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:25,602
Our welds use the cathodic
protection curve.

235
00:13:25,622 --> 00:13:29,885
Below the mudline, C203 allows it when the
structure above the mudline is

236
00:13:29,905 --> 00:13:31,235
cathodically protected.

237
00:13:33,067 --> 00:13:36,793
Now let's read the S-N curve properly.

238
00:13:36,813 --> 00:13:40,441
Equation 2.8 writes it in logarithms:

239
00:13:40,461 --> 00:13:43,103
log N = log ā − m · log Δσ.

240
00:13:43,723 --> 00:13:49,590
N is the number of cycles to failure at a
constant stress range Δσ, in megapascals.

241
00:13:49,610 --> 00:13:51,859
m is the negative inverse slope:

242
00:13:51,879 --> 00:13:57,676
on log-log axes, the curve drops one decade in
stress for every m decades in life.

243
00:13:57,696 --> 00:14:01,719
And log ā is where the design curve
meets the log N axis,

244
00:14:01,739 --> 00:14:03,634
at a stress range of 1 MPa.

245
00:14:04,254 --> 00:14:06,957
ā is not the average of the tests.

246
00:14:06,977 --> 00:14:13,043
Equation 2.9 shifts the mean curve down by
two standard deviations of log N.

247
00:14:13,063 --> 00:14:17,768
C203 uses a standard deviation of
0.2, so the shift is 0.4:

248
00:14:17,788 --> 00:14:22,654
the design curve allows 2.5 times
fewer cycles than the mean.

249
00:14:23,274 --> 00:14:25,793
Each curve has two branches.

250
00:14:25,813 --> 00:14:33,320
For the D curve in seawater with cathodic
protection, log ā₁ is 11.764, with m = 3,

251
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:35,406
up to a million cycles.

252
00:14:35,426 --> 00:14:39,215
Beyond that, log ā₂ is 15.606, with m = 5.

253
00:14:39,235 --> 00:14:43,750
The two branches meet at a
stress range of 83 MPa.

254
00:14:44,370 --> 00:14:46,479
Below the bend, cycles still count.

255
00:14:46,499 --> 00:14:50,069
Under variable amplitude loading
there is no fatigue limit:

256
00:14:50,089 --> 00:14:54,327
once the big cycles have started a crack,
the small ones help it grow.

257
00:14:54,347 --> 00:14:56,883
They just follow the shallower slope of 5.

258
00:14:57,503 --> 00:15:00,552
Then the thickness effect, equation 2.10.

259
00:15:00,572 --> 00:15:04,894
The stress range is multiplied by
t/t_ref, to the power k,

260
00:15:04,914 --> 00:15:06,915
before it enters the curve.

261
00:15:06,935 --> 00:15:11,332
t_ref is 25 mm, t is the thickness
the crack grows through,

262
00:15:11,352 --> 00:15:14,850
and walls thinner than 25 mm
get no correction.

263
00:15:14,870 --> 00:15:20,690
For the D curve, k is 0.2, so the 38 mm
conductor pays a 9% penalty on stress.

264
00:15:21,310 --> 00:15:22,502
Let's try it.

265
00:15:22,522 --> 00:15:29,683
A 60 MPa range at the girth weld becomes 65.3
after the thickness correction.

266
00:15:29,703 --> 00:15:34,440
That's below the bend, so we use
the second branch:

267
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:39,849
log N is 15.606, minus five times
log 65.3, which is 6.53.

268
00:15:39,869 --> 00:15:42,181
About 3.4 million cycles.

269
00:15:42,201 --> 00:15:48,336
A range of 100 MPa lands on the steep
branch, with 450,000 cycles.

270
00:15:48,356 --> 00:15:51,787
67% more stress, 7.5 times less life.

271
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:54,011
Step 5:

272
00:15:54,031 --> 00:15:54,812
adding it up.

273
00:15:54,832 --> 00:15:59,801
Palmgren-Miner's rule says that each stress
range uses up a fraction of the life:

274
00:15:59,821 --> 00:16:04,420
the number of cycles it occurs, divided by
the number the S-N curve allows.

275
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:06,945
The damage is the sum of those fractions,

276
00:16:06,965 --> 00:16:09,717
and failure is predicted when
it reaches one.

277
00:16:10,337 --> 00:16:13,485
Written out, this is equation 2.3:

278
00:16:13,505 --> 00:16:15,535
D is the sum of nᵢ/Nᵢ.

279
00:16:15,555 --> 00:16:19,729
From the S-N curve, Nᵢ is ā
divided by Δσᵢ^m.

280
00:16:19,749 --> 00:16:23,270
So D is 1/ā times the sum of nᵢ·Δσᵢ^m.

281
00:16:23,290 --> 00:16:26,531
That's why the big ranges dominate:

282
00:16:26,551 --> 00:16:30,818
each cycle counts with its
stress range cubed.

283
00:16:31,438 --> 00:16:36,690
With a two-slope curve, each range
goes to its own branch.

284
00:16:36,710 --> 00:16:42,507
A 69 MPa range is below the bend after
the thickness correction,

285
00:16:42,527 --> 00:16:45,507
so N comes from the m = 5 branch:

286
00:16:45,527 --> 00:16:47,234
1.7 million cycles.

287
00:16:47,254 --> 00:16:49,597
25 of them use 1.5 × 10⁻⁵.

288
00:16:49,617 --> 00:16:55,506
A range of 86 MPa lands on the steep
branch, with 700,000 cycles.

289
00:16:55,526 --> 00:16:59,869
C203 asks for at least 20
blocks in a histogram.

290
00:16:59,889 --> 00:17:05,595
We have 42, and together this 3-hour
sea state uses 1.9 × 10⁻⁴.

291
00:17:06,315 --> 00:17:10,519
We do this for every sea state, weight
each one by how often it occurs,

292
00:17:10,539 --> 00:17:12,898
and get the damage per day of operation.

293
00:17:12,918 --> 00:17:15,456
This map shows where the
damage comes from.

294
00:17:16,077 --> 00:17:17,783
The calm days hardly matter.

295
00:17:17,803 --> 00:17:22,962
The waves are below 2 metres half of the time,
and cause less than 1% of the damage.

296
00:17:22,982 --> 00:17:26,230
For the girth weld, the storms
don't dominate either:

297
00:17:26,250 --> 00:17:30,360
they are rare, and above 50 kN the
template takes part of the load.

298
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:35,169
Three quarters of the damage comes from
moderate seas, between 3 and 6 metres.

299
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:38,947
Section 5.4 offers a shortcut.

300
00:17:38,967 --> 00:17:43,865
If the long-term stress ranges follow a
two-parameter Weibull distribution,

301
00:17:43,885 --> 00:17:49,046
the damage has a closed-form solution, and you
don't need to count every cycle.

302
00:17:49,666 --> 00:17:55,495
Equation 5.1 gives the probability that
a stress range exceeds Δσ:

303
00:17:55,515 --> 00:17:57,178
Q = exp[−(Δσ/q)^h].

304
00:17:57,198 --> 00:18:03,470
q is the scale parameter, in megapascals,
and h is the shape parameter,

305
00:18:03,490 --> 00:18:06,926
which sets how fast the tail drops off.

306
00:18:06,946 --> 00:18:11,534
Fitted to our girth weld, h is
1.1, and q is 21 MPa.

307
00:18:12,154 --> 00:18:17,988
Equation 5.2 links q to the
largest range you expect.

308
00:18:18,008 --> 00:18:24,504
In n₀ cycles, the largest range,
Δσ₀, is q × (ln n₀)^(1/h).

309
00:18:25,124 --> 00:18:27,776
Now the damage, equation 5.3.

310
00:18:27,796 --> 00:18:31,369
ν₀ × T_d is the total number of cycles.

311
00:18:31,389 --> 00:18:37,910
q^m × Γ(1 + m/h), is the average of Δσ^m
over the Weibull distribution.

312
00:18:37,930 --> 00:18:41,503
So the closed form is just Miner's sum:

313
00:18:41,523 --> 00:18:46,939
the number of cycles, times the mean
of Δσ^m, divided by ā.

314
00:18:47,559 --> 00:18:53,621
With a two-slope S-N curve, equation 5.4 splits
the same average at the bend, S₁.

315
00:18:53,641 --> 00:18:59,403
The complementary incomplete gamma function
covers the ranges above the bend,

316
00:18:59,423 --> 00:19:00,529
with m₁ and ā₁.

317
00:19:00,549 --> 00:19:05,635
The incomplete gamma function covers
those below it, with m₂ and ā₂.

318
00:19:06,255 --> 00:19:07,127
For our weld:

319
00:19:07,147 --> 00:19:12,477
12,000 cycles a day, h of 1.1, and q of 23 MPa
after the thickness correction.

320
00:19:12,497 --> 00:19:17,484
The two-slope form gives 1,400 days, within
10% of the full rainflow sum,

321
00:19:17,504 --> 00:19:18,924
but on the long side.

322
00:19:18,944 --> 00:19:24,617
That's why C203 asks for a shape parameter on
the safe side when you use the closed

323
00:19:24,637 --> 00:19:24,960
form.

324
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:29,212
Use a single slope throughout, and the
life drops to 914 days:

325
00:19:29,232 --> 00:19:30,790
safe, but conservative.

326
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:33,044
Step 6:

327
00:19:33,064 --> 00:19:34,171
the design check.

328
00:19:34,191 --> 00:19:37,155
Even with conservative S-N
curves, the loads,

329
00:19:37,175 --> 00:19:40,006
the soil and the fabrication
are uncertain.

330
00:19:40,026 --> 00:19:43,453
So the design standard adds a
design fatigue factor:

331
00:19:43,473 --> 00:19:47,564
the calculated damage, times that
factor, must stay below one.

332
00:19:47,584 --> 00:19:51,741
In C203's terms, the damage must stay
below the usage factor η,

333
00:19:51,761 --> 00:19:54,658
which is one over the design
fatigue factor.

334
00:19:55,278 --> 00:19:59,315
The factor depends on whether a crack
could be found and repaired,

335
00:19:59,335 --> 00:20:01,281
and on what happens if it isn't.

336
00:20:01,301 --> 00:20:03,248
Typical values run from 1 to 10.

337
00:20:03,268 --> 00:20:06,813
A conductor 3 metres under the mud
can never be inspected,

338
00:20:06,833 --> 00:20:09,702
and losing it threatens the
well, so we use 10.

339
00:20:10,422 --> 00:20:14,602
Here are the results, in days of
riser-connected operation.

340
00:20:14,622 --> 00:20:18,303
The high-pressure housing weld is
far from critical.

341
00:20:18,323 --> 00:20:21,791
The conductor housing weld
allows about 240 days.

342
00:20:21,811 --> 00:20:26,631
And the governing hot spot is the plain
girth weld in the conductor:

343
00:20:26,651 --> 00:20:32,325
about 1,275 days of calculated life, or 128
days with the design fatigue factor.

344
00:20:33,044 --> 00:20:38,488
The drilling campaign needs 120 days with the
riser connected, so the well passes,

345
00:20:38,508 --> 00:20:41,153
but only just, at ninety-4% utilisation.

346
00:20:41,173 --> 00:20:43,152
And notice which weld governs.

347
00:20:43,172 --> 00:20:48,483
Not the big forgings, but an ordinary girth
weld, out of sight below the seabed.

348
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:53,617
Before trusting that answer,
ask what moves it.

349
00:20:53,637 --> 00:20:58,090
Life scales with stress to the power 3,
and 5 for the small cycles,

350
00:20:58,110 --> 00:21:00,827
so 10% more stress costs 30% of the life.

351
00:21:01,447 --> 00:21:04,062
With 6% to spare, fabrication matters.

352
00:21:04,082 --> 00:21:09,262
8 mm of misalignment instead of 6, and the
allowable time drops to 90 days:

353
00:21:09,282 --> 00:21:11,342
the well would fail the check.

354
00:21:11,362 --> 00:21:16,542
Weld the same joint on site, and it becomes
an E detail, with only 82 days.

355
00:21:16,562 --> 00:21:20,286
Welding and tolerances belong in
the fatigue analysis.

356
00:21:20,905 --> 00:21:22,095
And the environment:

357
00:21:22,115 --> 00:21:25,787
the same weld in free corrosion,
without cathodic protection,

358
00:21:25,807 --> 00:21:28,026
has only a little over half the life.

359
00:21:28,647 --> 00:21:32,062
So report the result together with the
assumptions that drive it,

360
00:21:32,082 --> 00:21:35,233
and check those assumptions against
what was actually built.

361
00:21:37,067 --> 00:21:39,107
So what if the margin is too thin?

362
00:21:39,127 --> 00:21:43,956
C203 gives you several levers, and they differ
a lot in how much you can rely on

363
00:21:43,976 --> 00:21:44,259
them.

364
00:21:44,879 --> 00:21:47,255
The first is the detail itself.

365
00:21:47,275 --> 00:21:53,361
Specify shop welding with the pipe rotated, so
the weld stays a D and not an E.

366
00:21:53,381 --> 00:21:55,371
And tighten the tolerance:

367
00:21:55,391 --> 00:22:01,478
with 4 mm of hi-lo instead of 6, the allowable
time grows from 128 to 192 days.

368
00:22:02,098 --> 00:22:07,794
Second, grind the weld flush on both sides,
and prove it free of defects.

369
00:22:07,814 --> 00:22:10,300
Appendix A then allows curve C1.

370
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:14,607
But the misalignment allowance in
the S-N data is gone:

371
00:22:14,627 --> 00:22:17,974
δ0 becomes zero, and the SCF
rises to 1.39.

372
00:22:17,994 --> 00:22:22,046
Even so, the allowable time grows
to about 450 days.

373
00:22:22,666 --> 00:22:27,432
Third, improve the weld toe, by grinding,
TIG dressing or hammer peening.

374
00:22:27,452 --> 00:22:32,809
Table 7-1 credits up to 3.5 times the life for
grinding, and 4 for hammer peening.

375
00:22:32,829 --> 00:22:38,054
But C203 advises keeping toe grinding as a
reserve rather than counting on it at

376
00:22:38,074 --> 00:22:43,037
the design stage, TIG dressing is not
recommended for general use in design,

377
00:22:43,057 --> 00:22:47,692
and hammer peening is only for members
where failure has no substantial

378
00:22:47,712 --> 00:22:48,545
consequences.

379
00:22:49,165 --> 00:22:53,892
None of this helps if the crack would start
at the root of a one-sided weld.

380
00:22:53,912 --> 00:22:56,265
And with a calculated life this short,

381
00:22:56,285 --> 00:22:59,700
under 15 years for a component
where failure is severe,

382
00:22:59,720 --> 00:23:03,510
C203 asks for a more refined
analysis, or fracture mechanics,

383
00:23:03,530 --> 00:23:05,197
before trusting the number.

384
00:23:05,217 --> 00:23:07,508
Often the best lever is the simplest:

385
00:23:07,528 --> 00:23:09,507
a lower stress range, by design.

386
00:23:11,333 --> 00:23:12,601
So that's the method:

387
00:23:12,621 --> 00:23:17,261
sea states, stress at the hot spots, rainflow
counting, the right S-N curve,

388
00:23:17,281 --> 00:23:19,775
Miner's sum, and a design fatigue factor.

389
00:23:20,595 --> 00:23:22,728
Every wave leaves a little damage behind.

390
00:23:22,748 --> 00:23:26,141
Count it carefully, and you'll know how
long the steel will last.

